# Wave protocol — restraint, and what a baseline-free scan may claim
`cleanup` takes its scope as an argument: by default the files changed on the
branch, and at `--scope=all` the whole codebase. That is the difference from
`audit`, and it is also the reason two rules written for `audit` matter more here
than there. This file carries both, restated for a skill that has no baseline.
## Why this file exists at all
The restraint rule and the delta/state split were first written for a
diff-shaped review tool — a PR reviewer, where the set of things it may say is
already bounded by the size of the change. `cleanup` has no such bound.
| | `audit` | `cleanup` |
|---|---|---|
| Input | A named target with a baseline | The files in scope: by default those changed since the merge base, at most the codebase entire |
| Output | A verdict | A work plan |
| Natural bound on output size | The diff | **none**: a scope bounds where to look, not how many findings it holds |
**The flooding risk is overwhelmingly larger here.** A rule that keeps a PR
review readable is a rule that keeps a whole-repository sweep from being useless.
## Restraint — no nit-flood
> Do not flood the review with nits. Prefer a small number of high-confidence
> findings.
A sweep that returns two hundred findings has not found two hundred problems; it
has moved the sorting work onto the reader and called that a result. The
findings that matter are then indistinguishable from the ones that do not,
which is the same outcome as reporting nothing, at greater cost.
### The output cap
**One run reports at most twenty findings.** When more qualify, the list is
truncated and the report states the truncation in the form:
```
Show N of M
```
Two rules govern the cut.
1. **What is cut is the lower priority, not the lower confidence.** Cutting by
confidence keeps the speculative findings and drops the certain ones, which
inverts the restraint rule it was meant to serve
2. **The count of what was cut is stated, never implied.** A silent truncation
turns a bounded report into a false claim of completeness
`scripts/cap_findings.js` enforces the cap so it does not depend on anyone
remembering it. It sorts by severity, keeps the top twenty, and reports how many
it cut.
## Delta axes and state axes
The taxonomy's axes divide by whether they need a baseline to mean anything.
| Delta axis — needs a baseline | State axis — needs none |
|---|---|
| Pushed a file from under 1000 lines to over | The file is over 1000 lines |
| Branch complexity **this change added** | Thin wrappers, identity abstractions |
| A cohesive module became **more** coupled | Feature logic leaking onto a shared path |
| The approval bar's five "the PR ..." items | Magic hiding a simple structure; casts and `any` that blur a contract |
| | Code judo — restructuring that removes the branch entirely |
**`cleanup` has no baseline, so the delta axes are void here.** Applying them
anyway produces a finding whose evidence does not exist: nobody can say what a
file's length was before, when there is no before.
The default `--scope=diff` is `base..HEAD`: the files the branch touched. That
chooses where to look. It does not give the sweep a before-state, because the sweep
does not compare those files with how they stood at the merge base, so the delta
axes stay void under it. What the branch itself added is `audit`'s question.
### Why the split is a burden question, not a bookkeeping one
The upstream rule this comes with is *presumptive blocker* — the default verdict
is refusal and the burden of proof sits with the author. In a PR that is exactly
right: the author is present, the change is theirs, and they can discharge the
burden.
Run the same rule over a whole repository with no baseline and the author is
gone. **The burden lands on whoever inherited the code**, for defects they did
not introduce, with no bound on how many. That is not a review; it is an
unbounded backlog handed to the wrong person.
The split is what stops this. State axes stand on their own evidence and can be
reported to anyone. Delta axes cannot, so here they are not reported at all.
## `cleanup` produces a plan, not a verdict
There is no baseline, so there is nothing to approve or refuse, so the vocabulary
of refusal does not apply. **The output of a `cleanup` sweep never uses a verdict word.**
It names what to change, under which axis, in what order — and the reader
decides.
This matches the survey form of the two output shapes: with a baseline, a
verdict; without one, findings ordered by priority and no verdict word anywhere.
## One wave, one axis
A wave is one pass over the selected scope applying exactly one axis.
| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| One axis per wave | A pass that deletes dead code *and* inverts a dependency cannot be half-reverted when one of them is wrong |
| At most twenty findings per wave | The cap is per run, and a wave is a run |
| Tests re-run after each wave, not at the end | A failure after five mixed waves does not say which wave caused it |
| A wave that cannot be locked is not run | Locking the behaviour comes before the first wave, not between waves |
The pass order in `SKILL.md` is the wave order. Removing swallowing fallbacks
comes before consolidating shared paths, because consolidating a fallback that
swallows promotes the defect onto the shared path and the same bug then reaches
every caller.
